@node Exceptions
@section Exceptions

This section describes Scheme's exception-handling and exception-raising
procedures.  For the concept of Scheme exceptions, @pxref{Error
situations and unspecified behavior}.  @xref{Exception handling} for the
@code{guard} syntax.

@define{Exception handlers} are one-argument procedures that determine
the action the program takes when an exceptional situation is signaled.
The system im@value{shy}pli@value{shy}citly maintains a current
exception handler in the dynamic environment.

@cindex current exception handler

The program raises an exception by invoking the current exception
handler, passing it an object encapsulating information about the
exception.  Any pro@value{shy}ce@value{shy}dure accepting one argument
can serve as an exception handler and any object can be used to
represent an exception.

@deffn procedure with-exception-handler handler thunk

It is an error if @var{handler} does not accept one argument.  It is
also an error if @var{thunk} does not accept zero arguments.

The @code{with-exception-handler} procedure returns the results of
invoking @var{thunk}.  @var{Handler} is installed as the current
exception handler in the dynamic environment used for the invocation of
@var{thunk}.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(call-with-current-continuation
 (lambda (k)
  (with-exception-handler
   (lambda (x)
    (display "condition: ")
    (write x)
    (newline)
    (k 'exception))
   (lambda ()
    (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))))
                               @output{condition: an-error}
                               @ok{exception}

(with-exception-handler
 (lambda (x)
  (display "something went wrong\n"))
 (lambda ()
  (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))
                               @output{something went wrong}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(call-with-current-continuation
 (lambda (k)
  (with-exception-handler
   (lambda (x)
    (display "condition: ")
    (write x)
    (newline)
    (k 'exception))
   (lambda ()
    (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))))
                               @ok{exception}
           @andprints{condition: an-error}

(with-exception-handler
 (lambda (x)
  (display "something went wrong\n"))
 (lambda ()
  (+ 1 (raise 'an-error))))
           @prints{something went wrong}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo

After printing,the second example then raises another exception.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure raise obj

Raises an exception by invoking the current exception handler on
@var{obj}.  The handler is called with the same dynamic environment as
that of the call to @code{raise}, except that the current exception
handler is the one that was in place when the handler being called was
installed.  If the handler returns, a secondary exception is raised in
the same dynamic environment as the handler.  The relationship between
@var{obj} and the object raised by the sec@value{shy}on@value{shy}dary
exception is unspecified.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure raise-continuable obj

Raises an exception by invoking the current exception handler on
@var{obj}.  The handler is called with the same dynamic environment as
the call to @nobr{@code{raise-continuable}}, except that: (1) the
current exception handler is the one that was in place when the handler
being called was installed, and (2) if the handler being called returns,
then it will again become the current ex@value{shy}cep@value{shy}tion
handler.  If the handler returns, the values it returns become the
values returned by the call to @nobr{@code{raise-continuable}}.

@ifinfo
@lisp
(with-exception-handler
  (lambda (con)
    (cond
      ((string? con)
       (display con))
      (else
       (display "a warning has been issued")))
    42)
  (lambda ()
    (+ (raise-continuable "should be a number")
       23)))
                              @output{should be a number}
                              @ok{65}
@end lisp
@end ifinfo
@ifnotinfo
@lisp
(with-exception-handler
  (lambda (con)
    (cond
      ((string? con)
       (display con))
      (else
       (display "a warning has been issued")))
    42)
  (lambda ()
    (+ (raise-continuable "should be a number")
       23)))
    @prints{should be a number}
                              @ok{65}
@end lisp
@end ifnotinfo
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error message obj @value{defdots}

@var{Message} should be a string.

Raises an exception as if by calling @code{raise} on a newly allocated
imple@value{shy}mentation-defined object which encapsulates the
information provided by @var{message}, as well as any @var{obj}s, known
as the @define{irritants}.  The procedure @nobr{@code{error-object?}}
must return @code{#t} on such objects.

@lisp
(define (null-list? l)
  (cond ((pair? l) #f)
        ((null? l) #t)
        (else
          (error
            "null-list?: argument out of domain"
            l))))
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object? obj

Returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is an object created by @code{error} or
one of an implementation-defined set of objects.  Otherwise, it returns
@code{#f}.  The objects used to signal errors, including those which
satisfy the predicates @code{file-error?} and @nobr{@code{read-error?}},
may or may not satisfy @nobr{@code{error-object?}}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object-message error-object

Returns the message encapsulated by @var{error-object}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure error-object-irritants error-object

Returns a list of the irritants encapsulated by @var{error-object}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure read-error? obj
@deffnx procedure file-error? obj

Error type predicates.  Returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is an object
raised by the @code{read} pro@value{shy}ce@value{shy}dure or by the
inability to open an input or output port on a file,
re@value{shy}spec@value{shy}tively.  Otherwise, it returns @code{#f}.
@end deffn
